A||||4||Ken Wittlief||How do you check the engine mounts?||||1||
Z||000000||Ken Wittlief||05-14-2001||09:38 AM||||I get a bit of a clunk from the rear when i accelerate hard and Im wondering how to check the engine mounts.<P> the rubber inside the dogbone looks okay, no tears or anything, and searching though the archives I saw the 'rocking the car in park' suggestion, so I will try that.<P>  but how do you check the ones underneigth?  If they are bad can you push the engine up with your arms or would you have to try to jack the engine up with the car on jack stands. This sounds dangerous, and it sounds like you could wreck good engine mounts if you put too much pressure on them with the jack while trying to test them.<P>   You really cant see them buggers underthere very well - If they were bad would they be obviously ripped? or do I need to push and pry on them with some serious fource to see if they will are separated.<P>  the V6 and tranny weigh what, about 600lbs? Is the clunk on hard acceleration enough evidence that I need to replace them all?||||reg||1
Z||000001||FieroLisa||05-14-2001||09:51 AM||||I had a coupe with every engine mount bad. You could rock the engine with your hands, no problem, my dogbone was shot, and the lower one on the left, closer to the trunk., had a big visible rip in it.<P><P>------------------<BR>Lisa<BR>86 violet pearl 4 speed GT: 17 in. rims, lowered, cold-air intake<BR>AOL IM: FieroLisa<P><A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/fierolisa/" TARGET=_blank>FieroLisa's Website</A>*<A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/ncfieros/" TARGET=_blank>NC Tarheels Fieros Site</A><P>    <IMG SRC="http://www.geocities.com/biohazard_racing/lisaonhood.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||84Bill||05-14-2001||10:08 AM||||The way I check it put a short 2x4 under the trans oil pan and jack it up just a little bit about an inch or so. Shine a light around the mounts and look for the brakes.||||reg||1
Z||000003||WKDFIRO||05-14-2001||04:26 PM||||Not sure just how accurate this would be but if you put the car in gear (or keep it in park) but do not put on the parking brake you should be able to see any odd movements when you push the car forward. <P>I would think that it would be the closest way other than starting it with the trunk open and having someone watch the engine. You could take off the dog bone to further isolate a possible motor mount. Usually its the front tranny mount that goes bad first. The torque pulling up on that mount is can be brutal especially on burnouts.<P>Hope this helps!||||reg||2
Z||000004||phreakboy4||05-14-2001||06:23 PM||||all you need to do is have someone watch the engine and if it is an auto hold the brake down and give it gas if the engine moves that some mounts need to be replaced, if it is stick just put blocks in front of tires and hit the gas<P>84 2M4 coupe                   Sean V||||reg||1
